@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · Vela UI</PageTitle>

<div class="flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-10">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h1 class="text-title text-fg">Getting started</h1>
        <p class="text-body text-fg-muted">
            Vela UI copies component source into your Blazor app, where it's yours to change. This
            sets up an app and adds a first component.
        </p>
    </header>

    <DocSection Title="What you need">
        <ul>
            <li>The .NET 10 SDK, and a Blazor Web App, Blazor WebAssembly app, or Razor class library on .NET 10 or later.</li>
            <li>Node.js 20 or later, to build the CSS with Tailwind CSS v4.</li>
            <li>
                A clone of the Vela UI repository. Until the <code>vela</code> command is published, you run
                it from there with <code>dotnet run</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="1. Set up your app">
        <p>In your project's folder, where its <code>.csproj</code> is:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Init" />
        <p>
            This writes <code>vela.json</code>, the theme (<code>Styles/vela.css</code>), a Tailwind input
            file, the fonts, the optional motion layer (<code>vela-light.js</code>), and
            <code>ClassNames.cs</code>. Then it prints the steps below, with the file names your
            project uses.
        </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="2. Build the CSS">
        <CodeBlock Code="@Tailwind" />
        <p>Leave the watcher running while you work, so new classes are picked up.</p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="3. Link the CSS and import the components">
        <p>In <code>Components/App.razor</code>, link the CSS in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and optionally load the motion layer before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Links" />
        <p>Then add the components' namespace to <code>Components/_Imports.razor</code>:</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Using" />
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="4. Add components">
        <CodeBlock Code="@Add" />
        <p>
            Each component goes to <code>Components/Ui/</code> with the components it uses. Some are
            built on the <code>Vela.UI.Primitives</code> package; until it's on NuGet, see
            <a href="troubleshooting#package">Troubleshooting</a>.
        </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="5. Use them">
        <CodeBlock Code="@Usage" />
        <p>
            A <code>Button</code> renders under static server rendering, but its <code>OnClick</code>
            needs an interactive render mode, such as <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code> on the
            page. Each component's page says what needs one.
        </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection Title="WebAssembly and Auto">
        <p>
            With the WebAssembly or Auto render mode, interactive components have to live in the
            <code>.Client</code> project, so run <code>vela init</code> and <code>vela add</code> there with
            <code>--cwd MyApp.Client</code>. <code>App.razor</code> and its stylesheet link stay in the
            server project, which serves the client project's files.
        </p>
    </DocSection>
</div>

@code {
    private const string Init =
        "dotnet run --project path/to/vela-ui/src/Cli -- init";

    private const string Tailwind =
        """
        npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/cli
        npx @tailwindcss/cli -i Styles/app.css -o wwwroot/tailwind.css --watch
        """;

    private const string Links =
        """
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="tailwind.css" />
        <script src="vela-light.js"></script>
        """;

    private const string Using = "@using MyApp.Components.Ui";

    private const string Add =
        "dotnet run --project path/to/vela-ui/src/Cli -- add button card";

    private const string Usage =
        """
        <Card Class="max-w-sm">
            <CardHeader>
                <CardTitle>Storage</CardTitle>
                <CardDescription>4.2 GB of 10 GB used.</CardDescription>
            </CardHeader>
            <CardFooter>
                <Button OnClick="ManageStorage">Manage storage</Button>
            </CardFooter>
        </Card>
        """;
}
